    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Oral mucositis is characterized by inflammation and ulceration in the oral mucosa due to chemotherapeutic treatment. An unintended primary target of this chemotherapeutic treatment is the junctional epithelium (JE), because of its high mitotic activity in the oral cavity. The JE is an essential epithelial barrier surrounding the surface of the tooth that rescues continuity of the oral epithelium (OE) when it is disrupted during tooth eruption. Despite its non-keratinized nature, the JE maintains its barrier function through hemidesmosomal attachment proteins and rapid cell proliferation. Here, we investigate the long-term effects of chemotherapeutic treatment on JE structure and function, focusing on the delivery of 5-Fluorouracil (5-Fu), a commonly used chemotherapeutic agent. To achieve this, we develop a novel topical delivery method for 5-Fu to mitigate systemic damage to the animals’ health. Our results demonstrated that topical 5-Fu application induced similar mitotic inhibition and tissue breakdown as systemic delivery, while minimizing global damage. However, long-term topical treatment surprisingly did not result in the complete loss of JE attachment to the tooth's surface. This challenges previous assumptions regarding the role of proliferation in mediating attachment. The study suggests the existence of a conserved attachment mechanism in the JE, potentially involving a separate Wnt signaling pathway. Future investigations using Wnt inhibitors and prolonged 5-Fu exposure aim to elucidate these mechanisms further. Additionally, the possibility of 5-Fu resistance or an accelerated repair response in the JE warrants continued exploration. Our study sheds light on the complex interplay between chemotherapeutic agents, JE biology, and tissue repair mechanisms, providing insights into potential therapeutic strategies for chemotherapy-induced oral mucositis and periodontal diseases.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
